<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hamilcar Barca</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 275–228 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcid-family">Barcid family</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hamilcar Barca was a Carthaginian general who commanded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in the last years of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-punic-war">First Punic War</a> and then crushed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary-war">Mercenary War</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. In 237 BCE he led an army to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, where he conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalquivir">Guadalquivir</a> valley and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines and founded a Carthaginian domain. He drowned in 228 BCE; his son-in-law Hasdrubal and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> continued his work.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hamilcar-barca.jpg" alt="Hamilcar Barca" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Max Karl Baldamus · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amilcare_Barca_(Ritratto).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Little is known of the Barca family before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-wars">Punic Wars</a>. One suggestion is that it came from Cyrene; another is that it belonged to the landed aristocracy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>. The epithet Barca, in Punic brq, means thunderbolt or shining. Hamilcar was the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>, Hasdrubal and Mago and the father-in-law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasdrubal-the-fair">Hasdrubal the Fair</a>. He was 28 when he received the command in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in 247 BCE, and he already had three daughters.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Hamilcar commanded the Carthaginian land forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> from 247 to 241 BCE with a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> army and little money. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> invested few men in the island, and his aim was probably to keep a stalemate. He fought a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, moved his army by night to the slopes of Mount Eryx in 244 BCE and held it for about three years, supplied by road from Drepana. When the Romans in 241 BCE demanded that he surrender his army's arms and Roman deserters, he refused, and the army left Sicily under arms.</p><p>The army then rebelled over unpaid wages in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary-war">Mercenary War</a>. After Hanno the Great failed to defeat the rebels, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> gave Hamilcar command of 10,000 men and 70 elephants. He won at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medjerda">Bagradas</a>, and when the rebels led by Spendius trapped him in a valley he was saved by the defection of the Numidian chieftain Naravas with 2,000 horsemen, who later married his third daughter. He then crushed the rebel army and crucified Spendius and other hostages outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>. Hamilcar allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasdrubal-the-fair">Hasdrubal the Fair</a> against Hanno's aristocratic party and avoided trial for having caused the war with unrealistic promises to his soldiers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 237 BCE Hamilcar led an expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasdrubal-the-fair">Hasdrubal the Fair</a> and the nine-year-old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>. Before he left Hannibal is said to have sworn never to be a friend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>; some modern historians read this as lifelong enmity, others call that a distortion. </p><p>Hamilcar secured the gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-morena">Sierra Morena</a>, defeated the Turdetani and a 50,000-strong army under Indortes, whom he crucified, and in four years of campaigns subdued the country between Gades and Cape Nao. He probably founded Akra Leuke, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alicante">Alicante</a>, in 235 BCE, and built a professional army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberians">Iberians</a>, Africans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numidians">Numidians</a> that Hasdrubal inherited and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> later led. When a Roman embassy came in 231 BCE he answered that he was gathering booty to pay <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>'s war indemnity.</p><p>He died in 228 BCE while besieging a town. Appian says he was thrown from his horse and drowned in a river; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> says he fell in battle against an unnamed tribe. A third version has the Oretani chieftain Orissus attack him as he retreated across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jucar">Júcar</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hamilcar Barca” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Hamilcar Barca</b>, then Hasdrubal the Fair, founder of Qart Hadasht (Cartagena), and from 221 BCE Hannibal conquered the Iberians south of the Ebro, and Hannibal’s siege of Saguntum, an ally of Rome, began the Second Punic War.</blockquote><a href="/culture/iberians">Iberians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>After losing Sicily in the First Punic War, Carthage sent <b>Hamilcar Barca</b> to Iberia in 237 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/barcid-iberia">Barcid Iberia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jucar">Júcar</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.85,10.31&amp;year=-245" aria-label="Carthage on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4809 836 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Hamilcar Barca"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5259 1106h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5259 1106h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.85,10.31&amp;year=-245">What was here in 245 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
